#
# conf/iocs.csv — hand-maintained indicators of compromise.
# Loaded by enrichment/inventory.py; INVENTORY_PATHS in .env points here.
#
# The header row must stay the FIRST line of the file so that a plain reader
# (csv.DictReader; a spreadsheet) sees the columns. Notes therefore live below
# the data. Columns in this order:
#   value       IP or domain; matched against the alert's srcip and dstip
#   reputation  malicious | suspicious | clean
#   expires_at  ISO-8601 UTC with a timezone; e.g. 2030-12-31T00:00:00Z
#   source      optional free text: where the indicator came from
#
# The enrichment query is
#   SELECT * FROM iocs WHERE value IN ($srcip,$dstip) AND expires_at > now()
# so a row whose expires_at has passed is invisible rather than wrong. validate()
# still reports it — prefixed `warning:` — so an expired row does not rot unseen.
#
# `not_found` and `skipped` are NOT the same (§6.2 output schema): a value that is
# simply absent from this file yields not_found; a lookup that was never performed
# yields skipped. G8' blocks auto-close on malicious and suspicious only, so
# neither not_found nor skipped blocks anything on its own.
#
# Blank lines and lines whose first non-space character is `#` are ignored
# (DEC-007), which is why these notes are safe to keep in the real conf/iocs.csv.
# A `#` inside a quoted field is part of the value, not a comment — see the
# suspicious row above.
#
# The example rows use the documentation ranges 203.0.113.0/24 and 198.51.100.0/24
# only. Never put a real address of the estate into an example file.
